# DTF tier manifest: shutdown-drain  (artifact-keeper#5921, 1.13.0)
# Profile-set: filesystem + lifecycle.shutdown-drain (passes
# SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECS through to the backend).
#
# THE PROBLEM (#5921)
# On SIGTERM the backend closed its HTTP listener at once. A load balancer or
# ingress learns that an endpoint is gone asynchronously (EndpointSlices reach
# ingress-nginx in about 1-2 s), so requests still sent to the terminating
# replica in that window were refused and the proxy answered 502, on every
# rolling update, scale-down and node drain. With one ready replica left the
# proxy's retries had no other peer.
#
# THE FIX (1.13.0)
# On SIGTERM the process marks itself draining: /readyz answers 503 with
# `shutting_down` in `failing`, /livez stays 200, background workers stop
# claiming new work. It keeps serving normally for SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECS
# (default 0, close at once; 5-10 s recommended behind a load balancer, so
# the tier sets 12), then closes the listeners, lets in-flight requests finish,
# waits for running scheduler jobs until the end of the drain, releases the
# scheduler leases it holds and exits 0. Each phase is logged.
#
# THE PRIMITIVE
# The oracle waits until the backend holds a live scheduler lease (the health
# monitor takes one 15-45 s after boot, TTL 90 s), starts one request that is
# held in flight by an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock on `users` (the oracle's own psql
# session; the anonymous downloads probed meanwhile do not read `users`),
# then sends SIGTERM to its own slot's backend container
# (`docker kill -s TERM ak-dtf<slot>-backend`) and probes. It ends the lock
# DRAIN + 3 s after SIGTERM, so the held request is still in flight when the
# listeners close, waits for the process to exit, checks the exit and the
# lease table, and starts the container again. Only this slot's containers
# are touched; an EXIT trap ends the lock and restarts the backend if the
# oracle dies midway.
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1), SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECS=12:
#   (CONTROL) before SIGTERM: /readyz and /livez 200, the anonymous download
#             of a public generic artifact 200 + bytes, a live scheduler lease
#             held by this backend.
#   (FIX #5921) within 1 s of SIGTERM /readyz answers 503 with
#             `shutting_down` in `failing`. RED on main: the port is closed
#             (curl 000).
#   (FIX #5921) /livez answers 200 during the drain. RED on main: 000.
#   (FIX #5921) from 1 s to 6 s after SIGTERM every download (one each
#             0.5 s) answers 200 with the bytes. RED on main: 000.
#   (FIX #5921) /livez still answers 200 at DRAIN - 1 s (11 s): the drain is
#             honoured. RED on main: 000.
#   (CONTROL) at DRAIN + 1 s (13 s) a new connection is refused (000) while
#             the held request is still in flight: the listener closed with a
#             request outstanding.
#   (CONTROL) the held request, released at DRAIN + 3 s, completes 200:
#             in-flight requests finish after the listener closes. The
#             process then exits with status 0, after the held request and
#             within 30 s of SIGTERM. After `docker start` it is ready again.
#   (FIX #5921) the log names each phase: "draining for 12s", "closing the
#             listeners", "shutdown complete". RED on main: none.
#   (FIX #5921) after the exit no scheduler lease owned by this backend is
#             still live. RED on main: the health monitor lease stays live
#             for its 90 s TTL.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false so a 429 can never masquerade as either verdict.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem lifecycle.shutdown-drain"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
COVERS="5921"
export SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECS="12"
